  </Part>

  <!--
    LAU-7 rail with one AIM-9J. The other shape a launcher takes: a part with nothing that moves,
    which attaches to the side of something else and shoots wherever that something is pointed.

    Geometry from tools/model/sidewinder.py. +X is out of the mounting face, +Y the way the
    missile points, +Z its right.
  -->
  <Part Id="KSArmory_Prefab_SidewinderRail">

    <SubPart Id="KSArmory_Rail_Beam" InstanceOf="KSArmory_SidewinderRail_SubPart" />

    <!-- Attaches by the pylon's base, which is the mesh origin. -->
    <Connector Id="_swConnectorBase">
      <Transform>
        <Position X="0.00000" />
        <Rotation Z="3.14159" />
      </Transform>
    </Connector>

    <!--
      The round, modelled where the mod will seat it rather than parked and shrunk away like the
      Pantsir's. A tube launcher hides its rounds inside the containers, so a body nobody has
      written yet is invisible either way; an open rail is empty. The editor runs no battery at
      all, and neither does the first frame of a flight, so this is the only thing that puts a
      missile on the rail in both.

      Position is the seated centre and the rotation is the quarter turn carrying the body mesh's
      nose from +X onto the tube axis - the same values TrySeatMissile computes, so nothing moves
      when the mod takes over. Keep in step with sidewinder.py's AXIS_X and BODY_LEN.
    -->
    <SubPart Id="KSArmory_Rail_Aim900" InstanceOf="KSArmory_Aim9_SubPart">
      <Transform>
        <Position X="0.40850" Y="0.00000" Z="0.00000" />
        <Rotation Z="1.57080" />
      </Transform>
    </SubPart>

  </Part>

  <!--
    14-inch ejector rack, radially mounted like the rail.

    The bomb hangs from it rather than being parked and shrunk away like the Pantsir's rounds: an
    open rack with nothing on it reads as broken, and the editor runs no fire control at all.
  -->
  <Part Id="KSArmory_Prefab_BombRack">

    <SubPart Id="KSArmory_Rack_Beam" InstanceOf="KSArmory_BombRack_SubPart" />

    <!-- Attaches by the pylon's base, which is the mesh origin. -->
    <Connector Id="_brConnectorBase">
      <Transform>
        <Position X="0.00000" />
        <Rotation Z="3.14159" />
      </Transform>
    </Connector>

    <!--
      Position is the seated centre and the rotation is the quarter turn carrying the body mesh's
      nose from +X onto the tube axis - the same values TrySeatMissile computes, so nothing moves
      when the mod takes over. Keep in step with bombrack.py's AXIS_X and BODY_LEN.
    -->
    <SubPart Id="KSArmory_Rack_Mk8200" InstanceOf="KSArmory_Mk82_SubPart">
      <Transform>
        <Position X="0.40650" Y="0.00000" Z="0.00000" />
        <Rotation Z="1.57080" />
      </Transform>
    </SubPart>

  </Part>

  <!--
    Phalanx-pattern CIWS, 4.7 m on a 3 m stack node.

    The first part here that STACKS rather than surface-attaching. A connector with no <Flags> is
    a node connector; ToSurface is the opt-in for radial. So this one sits on top of a tank, a
    decoupler or an adapter the way an engine sits under one, and the flange flares from the 3 m
    node to the mount's own 2.8 m base.

    One connector, at the bottom. Nothing stacks on top of a gun.

    Geometry from tools/model/ciws.py. +X is up the stack, +Y the way the gun faces at rest.
  -->
  <Part Id="KSArmory_Prefab_Ciws">

    <SubPart Id="KSArmory_Ciws_Base" InstanceOf="KSArmory_CiwsBase_SubPart" />

    <!--
      Traverses about the stack centreline. Its mesh is exported recentred on the pivot, so KSA
      spins it in place; the offset comes back here.
      Keep in step with TURRET_PIVOT in tools/model/ciws.py.
    -->
    <SubPart Id="KSArmory_Ciws_Turret" InstanceOf="KSArmory_CiwsTurret_SubPart">
      <Transform>
        <Position X="0.10000" Y="0.00000" Z="0.00000" />
      </Transform>
    </SubPart>

    <!--
      The whole head, meaning housing, barrels and the radome above them, swinging on the
      trunnion between the cheeks while riding the traverse. The mod rewrites both its rotation
      and its position every frame. The dome carries the track antenna, which stays boresighted
      with the barrels, so it is one rigid body with them rather than something the traverse
      holds upright while the barrels alone move.
      Keep in step with GUN_PIVOT in tools/model/ciws.py.
    -->
    <SubPart Id="KSArmory_Ciws_Guns" InstanceOf="KSArmory_CiwsGuns_SubPart">
      <Transform>
        <Position X="2.30000" Y="0.00000" Z="0.00000" />
      </Transform>
    </SubPart>

